      "answer": {
        "coverage": "complete",
        "insufficient_evidence": false,
        "summary": "Expert-led evals and post-training address a key AI-lab bottleneck: defining and measuring success so models can improve. Because labs need skilled professionals across domains and the work remains relevant as models advance, a marketplace that reliably supplies experts can serve substantial, continuing demand. Foody said Mercor’s revenue run rate grew from $1 million to $400 million in 16 months.",
        "summary_citation_ids": [
          "S1",
          "S2",
          "S3",
          "S6"
        ],
        "sections": [
          {
            "heading": "The work tackles a model-improvement bottleneck",
            "content": "Foody said labs’ primary bottleneck is measuring what success looks like for a model. Experts create evaluations and post-training data that help models focus on useful reasoning and capabilities; for example, radiologists can help define diagnoses and associated rewards or penalties. The labs are willing to spend heavily to improve model capabilities, creating strong demand for this work.",
            "citation_ids": [
              "S1",
              "S2"
            ]
          },
          {
            "heading": "Expert labor can deliver quality and scale",
            "content": "Foody described a large labor need—tens of thousands of people at a time and hundreds of thousands more broadly. He said higher-caliber workers can initially achieve more in smaller numbers than large volumes of low-skilled workers, with the workforce scaled up after meeting the quality bar. Much of the work involves longer-term projects, rather than one-off expert calls.",
            "citation_ids": [
              "S2",
              "S7"
            ]
          },
          {
            "heading": "Demand can persist as models improve",
            "content": "Foody called evals evergreen: labs need experts to define success across the domains they want models to handle, and to create post-training data for new capabilities. He argued that more thoughtful, data-efficient post-training—not simply much more pre-training data—will help advance capabilities, giving the work continuing relevance.",
            "citation_ids": [
              "S3",
              "S4"
            ]
          },
          {
            "heading": "Evidence of rapid growth",
            "content": "Foody reported that Mercor grew from $1 million to $400 million in revenue run rate in 16 months. The discussion links that growth to working with top AI labs and meeting their need for expert labor to measure and improve model capabilities.",
            "citation_ids": [
              "S1",
              "S6"
            ]
          }
        ],
        "missing_topics": []
      },
